@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the direction in which the measure is read
#
# One generalization and one protocol on it, formed from four attested claims in
# three transmissions: the French animal magnetism of the eighteen-twenties, the
# Thelemic instructions of nineteen twelve, and the anthroposophical manual of
# nineteen eighteen. The contact routes between the three are documented and
# nothing here rests on their independence; what the statement rests on is that
# three transmissions with a measure all do the same thing with its direction.
#
# What it is not. It is not pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, which is
# about a run that produced nothing and the party the emptiness is charged to.
# Here something did happen and it went the wrong way, which is the harder case
# for a transmission and the easier one for an examiner: an absent result has no
# direction, a measured one has, and a rule that fixes the direction in advance
# is a rule a reader can find without any run at all.
#
# It is also not pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment, which says that inner
# appearances are named as the chief obstacle to further progress. That is a
# caution against over-valuing a favourable sign. This is a rule for reading an
# unfavourable one, and the two stand at opposite ends of the same instrument.
#
# The counter-search required by SPEC §16 was carried over the compiled holding
# before this was written, in two questions, and it returned two cases. Both are
# in the notes of the protocol below and both shaped the wording: the second
# clause of the statement exists because of the first case, and the third clause
# because of the second.
#

pmc:TheDirectionOfTheMeasure a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The direction in which the measure is read"@en, "Die Richtung, in der das Maß gelesen wird"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission gives the practitioner something by which to judge whether the practice is working — a counted quantity, a bodily state, or a change in his own condition — the same corpus supplies a rule under which a change in the unfavourable direction is to be read as a mark that the practice is working, or as a mark of the practitioner's improved observation, and supplies no rule under which a change in the favourable direction would count against it. Where a transmission instead states a rate against a level fixed before the practice begins, the same passage does name a pattern of result that would count against the claim. And the re-reading is placed where the practitioner judges his own state: where the same corpus has one person judge the result obtained for another, it may name the identical device and reject it." ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ThelemaFractiCrescentesNonSuntCasus, pmc:DouleursCritiquesCommePreuve, pmc:SteinerVerkehrteSchulung, pmc:ThelemaFacilitasEstIndiciumErroris, pmc:ThelemaErrorInharmonicus ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The first witness, and the one that shows the device at its most defensible. The Thelemic exposition of nineteen twelve has the practitioner count the number of times his thought wanders, on his fingers or on a string of beads, with a notebook and a watch before him. It then rules that if the breaks seem to become more frequent instead of less frequent, the student must not be discouraged, for this is partially caused by his increased accuracy of observation; and it offers as its analogy that the introduction of vaccination raised the apparent number of cases of smallpox, because people began to tell the truth about the disease instead of faking. The reasoning is sound. A rising count on a self-administered measure really is ambiguous between worse control and better observation. What the passage does not supply is any condition under which the ambiguity would be resolved the other way, and its only exit is the assurance that control will soon improve faster than observation and that the improvement will then become apparent in the record — which moves the criterion to a later point that is not named."@en ;
    skos:note "The second witness is bodily and older. The magnetic instruction of the eighteen-twenties states that the operation often excites pain in the diseased part and renews old and slumbering pains, that these are the efforts nature makes to triumph over the malady, that they are transient, and that the patient always finds himself better after them. The patient is told that the pains prove the action is powerful, that they are necessary to subdue the disease, that he is to bear them without speaking of them to anyone, and that he is not even to ask the operator to calm them. The distinguishing mark offered — that critical pains are transient and followed by improvement — is retrospective, so the patient in pain on the tenth day cannot apply it at the moment he would have to."@en ;
    skos:note "The third witness is the change in condition, and it is the widest. The anthroposophical manual of nineteen eighteen names, in its own voice, precisely what an outside observer would report of a failed case — illusion, the loss of the hold on the sensory world, the inability to tell truth from error, and a change of character for the worse, so that one becomes fearful, envious, vain, arrogant and self-willed having had none of these properties before — and assigns all of it to a schooling undertaken wrongly or too early. An observer who reports exactly these things about a practitioner reports something the book has already classified as a departure from the method."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourth is the same instrument read from the other end, and it is what makes the statement's first clause more than a complaint about one passage. The same Thelemic exposition rules that the beginner to whom the described difficulties do not occur need not imagine he is meditating, since it shows merely that he is incapable of concentrating his mind in the very smallest degree; and that the apparent simplicity of the practices is such that he is likely to wonder what all the fuss is about and to think himself specially gifted. Difficulty is progress and ease is incapacity, so no reading of that measure tells against the practitioner's progress. Each rule is defensible alone; the pair closes the instrument."@en ;
    skos:note "The second clause, and it is the part that keeps the statement from being a statement about occultism in general. The same instruction of nineteen-nine that demands a written record as the only possible check upon error also states a chance rate — the right card once in seventy-eight times, and a correct suit twice in seven by calling trumps every time — and a threshold above it, and then names a pattern of result that is worthless even if the numbers came out: to call the Lord of Love for the Lord of Strife would show that you were getting nothing right. Where a level is fixed before the practice begins, the transmission does name an excluded outcome. The re-reading appears where the measure is the practitioner's own report of his own state, and not where it is a count against a number nobody can move."@en ;
    skos:note "The third clause came out of the counter-search and is the sharpest thing here. The corpus that supplies the second witness rejects the identical device by name — when its adherents use it about somebody else. Against the blind confidence of enthusiastic operators, it says, who believe their somnambulists infallible, suppose that remedies which did not succeed were not exactly followed, and regard remedies that did mischief as a necessary crisis: where the prescription appears unsuitable the operator is to object, to have the organs examined in succession, to ask the reasons for the choice, and to present the medicine to be touched and tasted. Nothing in that passage is retracted eight pages earlier where the patient's own pains are declared proof. The device is admitted for the practitioner judging himself and refused for the practitioner judging another, and the corpus does not notice the difference."@en ;
    skos:note "What the statement does not say. It says nothing about whether any of these practices does anything, and it imputes nothing to anyone's motives: the first witness is a piece of correct statistical reasoning applied without a stopping rule, which is harder to see than an evasion and is the reason it is entered first rather than last. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, and it can be settled by reading."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that gives its practitioner a measure of the kind described and states a value or a direction of that measure at which the practice is to be judged not to work — not a term at which to persevere longer, and not a rule assigning the shortfall to the practitioner, but a reading of the measure itself that counts against the practice. The corpora likeliest to carry it are the ones where a measure is kept for somebody else: the medical canon with its ten-day terms, the Ayurvedic and Greek surgical literature with its staged prognoses, and the alchemical assay literature, where the thing measured is a material and not a person. The last is the nearest, and it is already in the holding as pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient; whether an assay of an operation ever carries an unexcused negative reading is the first question to put."@en ;
    skos:note "The weakness of the derivation, stated because a reader would otherwise have to find it. Three of the five nodes come from two authors writing within a hundred years of each other in the same European occult milieu, and the contact routes between magnetism, theosophy, anthroposophy and Thelema are documented throughout this holding. So the agreement is not evidence of independent invention and nothing here claims it is. What a fourth witness would have to add is a transmission with no route to these three that keeps a counted measure at all — and a counted measure is itself rare in this holding, which is the honest reason the witnesses cluster."@en .

pmp:TheDirectionOfTheMeasureExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether any transmission with a measure lets its unfavourable direction count"@en, "Ob eine Überlieferung mit Maß die ungünstige Richtung zählen lässt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission supplies a measure of whether the practice is working, it supplies a rule for reading a change in the unfavourable direction as confirmation and no rule for the converse. Two questions were put to the compiled holding, not one: whether a transmission with such a measure states a reading of it that counts against the practice, which is the falsifying case the claim names; and whether a transmission supplies the re-reading in a place the claim's third clause does not allow, that is where one person judges the result obtained for another."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheDirectionOfTheMeasure ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that gives its practitioner a counted quantity, a bodily state or a change in his condition by which to judge the practice, and states a value or a direction of that measure at which the practice is to be judged not to work. A term at which to persevere longer does not meet it, and neither does a rule charging the shortfall to the practitioner: the reading of the measure itself has to go against the practice. Where such a passage stands in a corpus that elsewhere supplies the re-reading, the claim is not refuted but its first clause is reduced to a tendency and has to say so." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two searches over the compiled holding on 2026-09-28, then at the nodes returned. The first asked which transmissions carry a measure and a stopping rule keyed to it, searching every tradition file case-insensitively for: grows worse, the worse, worse instead, renounce, leave off, discontinue, desist, abandon the practice, give up the practice, aggravation, crisis, is the sign that it is working, proof that it works, counted as proof, in vain, wasted, of no benefit. Every hit was read in its place. It returned one stopping rule and the rule is cancelled in its own corpus: the magnetic instruction tells the patient to renounce the practice after some days and use ordinary medicine if he grows worse, and the paragraph immediately following removes that case, requiring careful attention before one can say with certainty that the disease is rendered worse and warning that a person might be deceived by appearances and renounce at the moment the practice is on the point of doing the most good. The node is pmc:PresqueUnMois and the cancellation is recorded there. It is not a refutation; it is the strongest stopping rule the holding has, and it lasts eight pages."@en ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The second search asked where the re-reading sits and returned the case that shaped the third clause. In the same corpus as the second witness, the device is named and rejected: against operators who suppose that remedies which did not succeed were not exactly followed and regard remedies that did mischief as a necessary crisis. The node is pmc:CautionAgainstTrustingThePrescription. This is a deviation and it is entered as one, because the claim as first drafted said the corpus supplies the re-reading and nothing about where. What the deviation shows is that the same corpus can hold the device and its refusal at once, sorted by who is being judged; and it stops short of the step that would settle anything, since the prescriptions go to a candid physician who is not to be put in consultation with the somnambulist, so no judgement is ever recorded against another in a form anybody could count."@en ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not searched: anything outside this holding. The claim is about a form of wording and the holding carries some ninety corpora, so the cheap search is the one that was run. A transmission outside it that refutes the claim would still refute it, and the falsifier above says where to look first."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-28, over the compiled holding at the head of this branch" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-28" ;
    skos:note "What the protocol establishes and what it cannot. It establishes by reading that in the three transmissions of this holding that supply a measure of the practitioner's own state, the unfavourable direction is provided for and the favourable one is not; and it establishes that the holding contains no transmission of that kind whose measure is allowed to count against the practice. It cannot establish that none exists, because the search was over this holding and the holding is thin in exactly the respect that matters: a counted measure is rare in it, and three of the five nodes come from two authors in one milieu."@en ;
    skos:note "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is the one named in the second counter-search note. It is recorded as a deviation rather than folded into the statement because a claim is corrected where it is examined: the third clause of the statement carries the correction, and the protocol beside it carries the case that forced it. A later reader should be able to see that the clause was not there first."@en ;
    skos:note "No modern work is cited under pm:evidenceFrom and none was read. The claim is about what transmissions say and not about what happens to practitioners, so the evidence layer is not where it would be settled — but the neighbouring empirical question is worth naming for whoever wants it: whether a self-administered count of lapses in attention rises or falls over the first months of such a practice has been measured outside this holding, and a protocol that cited such work would be testing a different claim than this one."@en .